// Flight, aim, and camera tuning.
//
// These numbers carry over from `pewpew/sunweasel/rail/constants.js`, where they
// represent a great deal of feel iteration and are the most valuable thing in the old
// codebase after the design docs. They are reproduced here rather than re-derived.
//
// ⚠️ UNITS CHANGED. The old engine ran on a variable millisecond delta, so every rate
// was expressed PER MILLISECOND. This simulation runs on a fixed timestep in SECONDS,
// so every rate is multiplied by 1000 on the way in. Angles and distances are
// unitless or in world units and carry over untouched.
//
//   old (per ms)          new (per s)
//   BOX_MOVE     0.004  → 4      units/s
//   AIM_RATE     0.002  → 2      rad/s
//   CATCH_K      0.006  → 6
//   RET_K        0.010  → 10
//   HEAD_FWD_K   0.0022 → 2.2
//   ROLL_LEVEL_K 0.004  → 4
//
// Getting that conversion wrong is the single easiest way to silently change how the
// game feels, so it is spelled out rather than left implicit.

// ── Box translation vs. turning ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// These used to be two halves of ONE input: steering both slid the ship and turned the
// reticle, and `BOX_MOVE : AIM_RATE` was that ratio. They are now separate axes on the
// input frame — translation from `steerX/steerY`, rotation from `aimX/aimY` — and a
// single-stick source such as a keyboard recreates the old coupling by sending the
// same values to both. So the ratio still describes how a keyboard feels, and no
// longer constrains a thumb.

/**
 * Box slide rate at full stick, world units per second.
 *
 * Raised from 4 after flying it on a phone. The box is 8 wide, so at 4 u/s crossing it
 * took two full seconds — sluggish with a keyboard, and unusable with a thumb, where
 * sliding out of the way IS the defensive move. At 7 a full-width dodge takes about
 * 1.1s.
 *
 * This moves the golden replay hashes: every recorded run puts the ship somewhere
 * slightly different. It is a deliberate handling change, and it is the one number to
 * put back first if the flight feel goes wrong.
 */
export const BOX_MOVE = 7;
/** Reticle turn rate at full stick, radians per second. The reticle leads the ship. */
export const AIM_RATE = 2;

// ── Aim cones ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// How far off the rail's travel direction the reticle may sit. Kept small for the
// coupled (move + turn) cases. An omni stop ignores these and opens the full sphere.

/** Free aim while on the track. */
export const CONE_ANGLE = 0.6;
/** "Forward" mode and forward stops — tight, mostly-forward. */
export const FWD_CONE = 0.28;
/** An omni stop: the whole sphere, including backward. */
export const OMNI_CONE = Math.PI;

// ── Lead / lag / auto-level rates (exponential, per second) ──────────────────

/** Ship heading eases toward the reticle. This lag IS the aircraft feel. */
export const CATCH_K = 6;
/** Reticle eases back onto the heading when the stick is released. */
export const RET_K = 10;
/** Heading auto-levels to rail-forward when hands-off. */
export const HEAD_FWD_K = 2.2;
/** Wings re-level toward world-up when hands-off and away from the pole. */
export const ROLL_LEVEL_K = 4;
/**
 * Skip wing re-levelling within this |up ⟂ head|² of vertical.
 *
 * The re-level target reverses sign across the pole, so applying it there
 * reintroduces exactly the discontinuity the transported up-vector exists to avoid.
 */
export const POLE_GUARD = 0.35;

// ── Reticle ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/** How far ahead along the aim ray the reticle sits. */
export const RETICLE_DIST = 8;
/** Reticle grows with heading↔aim divergence — the lag reads as aim uncertainty. */
export const RETICLE_GROW = 0.9;

// ── Chase camera (third-person) ──────────────────────────────────────────────

export const CHASE_BACK = 5;
export const CHASE_HEIGHT = 2.4;
export const CHASE_AHEAD = 9;

// ── Spawning ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/** Seed new hazards this far ahead of the ship along the rail. */
export const LEAD_DIST = 20;
/** Anything closer than this AND within the view cone would pop in. */
export const SPAWN_MIN_DIST = 14;
/**
 * Half-angle of the cone around the view direction that spawns must avoid.
 *
 * Replaces the old exact camera-frustum test, which `sim` cannot perform (it has no
 * camera) and which rejection-sampled a VARIABLE number of RNG draws — enough on its
 * own to desync every multiplayer client. A cone is a conservative approximation of a
 * frustum: it excludes a little more than necessary, deterministically.
 */
export const SPAWN_VIEW_CONE = 0.9;

// ── Travel box default ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Authored per world; this is the fallback when a rail does not specify one.

export const DEFAULT_BOX_WIDTH = 6;
export const DEFAULT_BOX_HEIGHT = 4;
